mscoree: load the ARM64 Mono runtime on ARM64EC, and fetch the package that has one.

Wine Mono has a native ARM64 build and this tree was asking for the x86-64 one.

metahost.c picks the runtime's filename at compile time, and the arm64 case was
folded in with x86-64:

    #elif defined(__x86_64__) || defined(__aarch64__)
        libmono2_arch_dll = "\bin\libmono-2.0-x86_64.dll"

That was correct when it was written, because no ARM64 build of Mono existed.
It is wrong now, and on ARM64EC it is wrong twice over: Arm64EC compiles with
x64's preprocessor definitions -- Microsoft's ABI documentation is explicit that
_M_AMD64 and _AMD64_ are defined -- so an ARM64EC build does not even reach the
__aarch64__ half of that condition. It matches `defined(__x86_64__)` and asks
for the AMD64 runtime.

Loading that runtime means calling an x86-64 DllMain from ARM64EC code, and the
loader has no path for it: call_dll_entry_point falls through to a direct branch,
the CPU decodes x86-64 bytes as ARM64 instructions, and the module dies at
0xc000001d before it runs a line. Six attempts at building that path are recorded
in native/pins.env and none of them worked.

None of it was necessary. wine-mono 11.3.0, published 2026-08-17, added "a new
port of Mono to ARM64 Windows" -- libmono-2.0-arm64.dll, a genuine ARM64 PE,
machine 0xaa64. It runs natively: no emulator, no exit thunk, no dispatch
helper, and nothing for the loader to special-case.

So the ARM64EC case is tested first and names the ARM64 runtime, and appwiz.cpl
downloads the arm64 package rather than the x86 one. The version moves 10.4.1 ->
11.3.0 because 11.3.0 is the first release that has an arm64 .msi at all; 10.4.1
published one package, x86, holding the i386 and AMD64 runtimes only.

The arm64 package is a superset rather than an alternative: it carries
libmono-2.0-arm64.dll, -x86_64.dll and -x86.dll together, so a 32-bit .NET
program under WoW64 still finds the runtime it needs. Only the name mscoree
reaches for first has changed.

**The download had to be fixed too, and that bug is upstream's.**
append_url_params built the redirector's query string with GECKO_ARCH hardcoded,
for both addons. That was invisible while the two wanted the same value and is
not any more: Gecko has no ARM64 build and asks for x86_64, Mono has one and asks
for arm64. winemono.php honours what it is sent -- ?arch=x86_64 answers with
wine-mono-11.3.0-x86.msi -- so Mono fetched the x86 package, failed the SHA-256
check against the arm64 digest, and told the user the file was corrupt. The file
was fine; the request was wrong. The architecture now lives on the addon.


--- a/dlls/appwiz.cpl/addons.c
+++ b/dlls/appwiz.cpl/addons.c
@@ -56,10 +56,24 @@
 #define GECKO_SHA "???"
 #endif
 
-#define MONO_VERSION "10.4.1"
-#if defined(__i386__) || defined(__x86_64__) || defined(__aarch64__)
+/* Vessel: 11.3.0 rather than 10.4.1, because 11.3.0 is the first release with an
+ * ARM64 build in it -- "a new port of Mono to ARM64 Windows", published
+ * 2026-08-17. 10.4.1 shipped one .msi, x86, and the x86 package holds the
+ * i386 and AMD64 runtimes only; on this target the AMD64 one is what mscoree
+ * would pick and it cannot be loaded, because Wine's loader has no ARM64EC path
+ * for calling an x86-64 DllMain. A native ARM64 runtime needs none of that.
+ *
+ * **The arm64 package is a superset, not an alternative.** It carries
+ * libmono-2.0-arm64.dll, -x86_64.dll and -x86.dll together, so a 32-bit .NET
+ * program under WoW64 still finds the runtime it needs; only the name mscoree
+ * reaches for first changes, and metahost.c is where that is decided. */
+#define MONO_VERSION "11.3.0"
+#if defined(__arm64ec__) || defined(__aarch64__)
+#define MONO_ARCH "arm64"
+#define MONO_SHA "b0c47d374efaf22cbff76cb10488ac2d1627b067e085aef7acec573884120e0f"
+#elif defined(__i386__) || defined(__x86_64__)
 #define MONO_ARCH "x86"
-#define MONO_SHA "071f4b2887e1c97a11d791ff3d65be9429eed6dec4c2708888bfd546ba358e23"
+#define MONO_SHA "df2dfc1665c2511882e7cabd56eafd0c0a3d94e5a7e86f969277f6c189d418d3"
 #else
 #define MONO_ARCH ""
 #define MONO_SHA "???"
